The '''Volkswagen Golf''' ({{Audio|GT VW-Golf.ogg|listen}}) is a [[compact car]]/[[small family car]] ([[C-segment]]) produced by the German automotive manufacturer [[Volkswagen]] since 1974, marketed worldwide across eight generations, in various body configurations and under various [[nameplate]]s – including as the '''Volkswagen Rabbit''' in the United States and Canada (Mk1 and Mk5), and as the '''Volkswagen Caribe'''<ref>{{Cite web |title=Historia del VW Golf |url=https://www.dasweltauto.com.mx/es/blog/historias/evolucion-golf-caribe-volkswagen.html |access-date=2022-05-19 |website=www.dasweltauto.com.mx |language=es-MX |archive-date=28 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210728233514/https://www.dasweltauto.com.mx/es/blog/historias/evolucion-golf-caribe-volkswagen.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> in Mexico (Mk1). The Golf is Volkswagen's bestseller and the third [[List of best-selling automobiles|best-selling automobile of all time]], with over 37 million units sold worldwide.<ref name=VWG30mi>{{cite web|url=http://www.greencarcongress.com/2013/06/wolfsburg-20130614.html|title=30 millionth VW Golf rolls off assembly line in Wolfsburg |publisher=Green Car Congress|date=14 June 2013|access-date=2013-06-14}}</ref><ref name="carmagazine.co.uk">{{cite web | first=Gareth | last = Kent |title=VW Golf build passes 25 million |work= CAR magazine |date=30 March 2007 |url= http://www.carmagazine.co.uk/News/Search-Results/Industry-News/VW-Golf-build-passes-25-million-/?&R=EPI-3666 |access-date=11 March 2008}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Golf turns 45 – On 29 March 1974, Volkswagen started making Europe's most successful car |url=https://www.volkswagen-newsroom.com:443/en/press-releases/golf-turns-45-on-29-march-1974-volkswagen-started-making-europes-most-successful-car-4788 |access-date=2022-06-11 |website=Volkswagen Newsroom |language=en}}</ref>

The original Golf Mk1 was a front-engined, [[front-wheel drive]] replacement for the [[air-cooled]], rear-engined, rear-wheel drive [[Volkswagen Beetle]].

Initially, most Golfs were [[hatchback]]s, with the three-door version being somewhat more popular than the five-door.<ref>Figures for 1974-1987: 4,700,000 3-door models, 2,550,000 5-door models, according to: {{cite book|title=Deutsche Autos 1945–1990, vol.3|last=Oswald |first=Werner|year=2001|publisher=Motorbuch Verlag|location= Stuttgart|isbn=3-613-02116-1|pages=86–87}}</ref> Other variants include an [[Station wagon|estate]] (Variant, from 1993), [[convertible]] (Cabriolet or Cabrio, from 1979), and a Golf-based [[Sedan (car)|saloon]] called the [[Volkswagen Jetta|Jetta]], [[Volkswagen Vento|Vento]] (from 1992), or [[Volkswagen Bora|Bora]] (from 1999). The Golf covers economy to high-performance market segments.

The Golf has won awards, including the [[World Car of the Year]] in 2009, with the [[Volkswagen Golf Mk6|Mk6]] and in 2013 with the [[Volkswagen Golf Mk7|Mk7]]. Along with the [[Renault Clio]] and the Vauxhall Astra, the Golf is one of only three cars to have won European Car of the Year twice, in 1992 and 2013.<ref>[http://www.automotoportal.com/article/Top_5_Worlds_Most_Successful_Cars_Ever "Top 5 World's Most Successful Cars Ever"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200812065138/http://www.automotoportal.com/article/Top_5_Worlds_Most_Successful_Cars_Ever |date=12 August 2020 }}, Automotoportal.com</ref><ref name="coty">{{cite web |url=http://www.caroftheyear.org/previous-winners/1992_1/coty |title=Previous Winners |language=es |publisher=Car of the Year |access-date=9 November 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111031044912/http://www.caroftheyear.org/previous-winners/1992_1/coty |archive-date=31 October 2011 |df=dmy-all }}</ref> The Golf has made the annual [[Car and Driver 10Best]] list multiple times. The Mk7 won the [[Motor Trend Car of the Year]] award in 2015, and the [[Volkswagen Golf Mk1|Mk1]] GTI also won the award in 1985. The [[Volkswagen Golf Mk4|Mk4]] won for the best-selling car in Europe in 2001.
